From 9a89b7a5679007248764215dd08ced8b20dc447a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 10:46:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/22] sycl: PTQ1_0 reorder layout and a row-per-lane mat-vec

PR #235's PTQ1_0 mat-vec gives one lane a whole 128-weight block: 32 dp4a in series,
each after ~20 integer ops of base-3 decode, with lanes reading activations 144 B
apart. On an Arc Pro B70 it read 5120x17408 at 54 % of the memory bound when the
weight sat in the 24 MiB L2, and 37 % when it did not.

On the first single-token mat-vec a PTQ1_0 weight now moves, in place, to a layout
that tiles its rows in groups of 16 (ggml_sycl_reordered::ptq1_0_layout): block b
of a group is 7 planes of 16 words, so a sub-group takes one row per lane and
reads each plane as one 64-byte line. The 28-byte block is exactly 7 words: 6 of
qs, kept as they are, and one holding qh's 8 trits as 2-bit codes beside d. Every
lane then dots the same activations, which the sub-group reads once per block and
broadcasts by constant lane. Two bytes decode per 32-bit multiply (even and odd
bytes in 16-bit lanes), trits are dotted as 0..2, and the -1 offset folds into the
q8_1 sums. A short last row group is handled, and the sub-groups of a work-group
split a group's blocks when rows are few. One launch serves 1-8 columns.

Prefill against a reordered weight dequantizes it through a shape-aware function,
since the layout needs the row length; the element-count getters return nullptr
for a reordered PTQ1_0 weight instead of reading it as blocks.

B70, 5120x17408, n = 1: hot (test-backend-ops perf) 59.0 -> 28.6 us; with every
weight read from memory (8 distinct weights, up/down in turn) 87.6 -> 40.7 us.

On top of the PQ2_0/PTQ1_0 XMX path (#294), which rewrites the same weights
into its own layout in place, a weight takes whichever layout it gets first:
the XMX gate declines a weight this reorder already moved, and the reorder
skips a weight already in the XMX layout.
